WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.836
-Our next guest is
a talented actor you know from

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:05.839
films such as "An Education,"
"Shattered Glass," and "Jackie."

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.640
He's currently starring
in the fantastic

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.308
limited series "Dopesick."

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.711
The series finale will be
streaming tomorrow on Hulu.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.279
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.048
-You guys holding out on me?

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:18.184
-Um...

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.719
Well, it's still very early,

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.756
but we've begun looking at
something that could be big.

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:24.557
-What are we talking about?

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:26.359
-OxyContin.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:30.063
-Specifically, Purdue Pharma
is the company that makes it.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:32.999
They've been marketing the drug
and pushing it on doctors

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:36.169
as something that's nonaddictive
when it clearly is.

00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:37.403
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:39.238
our friend Peter Sarsgaard,
everybody.

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:41.007
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:48.114 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:55.187 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:56.689
Welcome back.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:58.624
-Thank you.
-It's so nice to see you.

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.727
-It's nice to be back
with a bunch of people.

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:02.194
-Yeah. Right? People are great.
-All in a little space

00:01:02.194 --> 00:01:05.031
like this.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:07.233
I like people.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.635
-We haven't seen each other
for years.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.437
-You're not going to
remember this,

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:14.974
but I think it was in 2015,
you know,

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.776
the race in Chilmark
down Middle Road.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.078
-Yep, this is a 5K.

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:21.046
-It's a 5K.
-5K in Martha's Vineyard.

00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:23.516
-And I used to be a decent
runner before I turned 50.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:26.185
-I remember that about you.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:28.287
Are you aware that
we raced each another?

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:29.789
-No.
-You're not aware of that.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:30.890
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.091
So, at the start,

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:33.993
I didn't realize you were
a runner at the time.

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:36.262
And I was like
a pretty decent runner.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.665
So I line up, and you're like --

00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:40.934
We're pretty close -- I don't
know why you didn't see me.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.436
I really saw you.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.604
You're kind of like over there
and I think, like, "Right.

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:48.808
I'm just going to --
That's my mark, you know?"

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:51.511
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.846
By the end of this race --
It's a road that's --

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:55.614
It's basically straight
the whole way,

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:58.517
so you can see a quarter a mile
ahead of you,

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:00.853
which is nice, and that way,
I could see you finish.

00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:03.022
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.491
-Because this guy's real fast.
Are you still really fast?

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:07.293
-I'm not very fast, no.

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:10.262
I still run, but I think
the thing is happening to me

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:11.731
that maybe had happened to you.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.566
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.568
I think I'm just
a few years behind you,

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.670
is what I'm getting at.
-I understand that.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.040
I'm having trouble sitting right
now, so let's get on with it.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.142
-The most depressing thing that
happened to me one year

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:26.411
when I ran that road race,
where I set a time

00:02:26.411 --> 00:02:27.713
that I was aiming to hit.

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:29.181
And then, you know,
they have photos.

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:32.384
My wife took a picture of me
finishing, and I had to crop it

00:02:32.384 --> 00:02:35.220
because in front of me finishing
like 50 feet ahead

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.422
was like a 9-year-old.

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.157
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:39.792
And I'm like sweating,

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.728
and it's just like
the happiest 9-year-old.

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:43.762
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.762 --> 00:02:45.665
I want to talk about this show
because it's so wonderful.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:47.199
But you had
an interesting experience.

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:49.302
Your wife Maggie Gyllenhaal,
a fantastic actor,

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:50.869
has now become --
[ Light applause ]

00:02:50.869 --> 00:02:54.007
Yeah, give it up for Maggie.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:58.077
-A very -- A very accomplished
director.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.180
She has a film coming out
that is getting a great deal

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.549
of Oscar buzz that you're in.

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:04.984
But I want to ask about
a short film

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:06.452
you were in called "Penelope."

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.687
Because it seems to me
that she gave you a role

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:10.957
that maybe you would only offer
to a husband.

00:03:10.957 --> 00:03:14.293
-Right. So, this is right at
the beginning of the pandemic.

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.596
I think we shot it
in April of 2020

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:18.798
on our property in Vermont.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:25.004
And it's -- it feels, you know,
post-apocalyptic sort of thing.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:28.341
But one of the features
of the show is

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:30.176
that I do make love to a tree.

00:03:30.176 --> 00:03:31.377
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:34.647
And it builds up to it
in a very natural way

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:35.848
and it totally makes sense.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:37.416
-You're not just like a creep
who humps a tree.

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:39.785
-I'm dropping this on you
right now, but, like,

00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:42.522
it actually --
you feel for both of us.

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.256
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:47.126
But this is on our actual
property that we shot it.

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.995
So since then, you know,
I've been back up there,

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:51.964
doing my little chores
and all of that.

00:03:51.964 --> 00:03:54.100
Every time I walk past
this tree...

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.400
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.603
...I have this kind of...

00:03:57.603 --> 00:03:59.238
It's like I can't look at
the tree,

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:00.740
the tree can't really look
at me.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.708
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.708 --> 00:04:07.647
So, yeah, but it's really lovely
being directed by your wife.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:08.915
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:11.651
-Do you think Maggie ever, like,
looks out the window

00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:15.855
and sees you looking at the tree
and thinks, "What have I done?"

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:17.990
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.626
-Maybe, maybe, maybe.

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:24.397
But, yeah, and then I did this
inc-- We had this

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:27.533
incredible experience on
"The Lost Daughter," where,

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:31.337
again, one of the first films
out in COVID, in August of 2020.

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:35.274
We went to a Greek island called
Spetses, which is incredible.

00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:38.911
We all quarantined for 14 days,
and we made this movie

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:43.982
end of September, October,
and incredible, incredi--

00:04:43.982 --> 00:04:47.420
I really, truly love
being directed by my wife.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.422
-That's really awesome.
I can't wait to see it.

00:04:50.422 --> 00:04:51.624
I've just heard
such incredible things,

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:53.225
and it seemed like
such an incredible cast.

00:04:53.225 --> 00:04:56.695
-Absolutely. Olivia Colman
and Dakota Johnson

00:04:56.695 --> 00:04:59.832
and Jessie Buckley and Ed Harris
and all these people.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.335
-"Dopesick" --
also an incredible cast.

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:04.203
This is a very --
This is a real story?

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:06.839
-Yeah.
-This is a story you were

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:07.973
familiar with before you did it.

00:05:07.973 --> 00:05:10.176
You'd actually done
a documentary

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:11.844
about the opioid crisis.
-Yes.

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:17.015
So, I have someone very close
to me who's really struggled

00:05:17.015 --> 00:05:19.017
with this for a very long time.

00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:23.156
And so this was back in 2016,
and Norman Lear

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:26.659
and Shonda Rhimes came to me
and said that they were doing

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:29.128
this series called
"America Divided,"

00:05:29.128 --> 00:05:31.163
which was about issues
leading up to the election

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:35.367
that maybe voters
should think about.

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:37.670
Now it seems like
very Pollyannaish to think

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.304
that issues are why people vote.

00:05:39.304 --> 00:05:43.242
[ Laughter ]
But, you know, I noted that

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:46.145
the opioid crisis was not on
the list of things,

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.180
and actually, Rosario,
who's in the show,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.816
did one about Flint, Michigan,
and the water crisis.

00:05:50.816 --> 00:05:54.186
And so they sent me
to Dayton, Ohio.

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:56.989
And I went on ride-alongs
with police officers.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.524
I hung out with people
who were using --

00:05:58.524 --> 00:06:00.993
actively using in front of me.

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:03.663
Family members. Support groups.

00:06:03.663 --> 00:06:06.933
Went to the jail and saw users
trying to dry out.

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:11.737
And it actually gave me
a different perspective

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:13.639
on my own personal situation

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.608
and had me have a different type

00:06:15.608 --> 00:06:18.811
of understanding,
empathy, patience,

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.012
all of the things
you're going to need

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.981
if you're around somebody
who's going through this.

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:25.751
And so when this show came
along, I was working with Warren

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:27.286
on something else --
Littlefield,

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.720
who's the producer of this.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:30.489
And I was like, "I have to be
a part of that.

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:31.924
I don't care what role it is."

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.360
And then they offered me a role
of, like, a decent person.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.328
I assumed they would offer me
the role of, like,

00:06:36.328 --> 00:06:37.763
somebody slinging dope.

00:06:37.763 --> 00:06:39.865
[ Laughter ]

00:06:39.865 --> 00:06:41.867
The fact that I got to play,
like, you know,

00:06:41.867 --> 00:06:44.870
a self-righteous, good guy
is awesome.

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:48.340
-You also got to work with
the great Michael Keaton,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.411
who is not only a fantastic
actor, but he was somebody

00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:54.646
I've been lucky enough to get
to meet a couple times.

00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:56.515
And it was deeply important to
me that Michael Keaton

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:58.351
was a good person,
which he also is.

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:01.754
-He also is, and I think we were
talking about this backstage,

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:05.958
that Michael Keaton actually
never begins or ends a sentence.

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:07.627
-Yeah.
-He only does the middle part.

00:07:07.627 --> 00:07:09.328
[ Laughter ]

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:14.500
And we've been doing all this
publicity, and I just --

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.136
I just love the way, you know --
he's like, you know,

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:20.772
"And I was -- you know, it's --
I got the Tesla

00:07:20.772 --> 00:07:24.710
'cause I'm a knee-jerk --
you know, no one wants to hear

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.845
an actor talk about this kind of
stuff, but it's --

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:28.247
you know, I got the little one.

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:30.950
I got just the little --
you know, it's --"

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:34.620
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.089
"I don't know."
-[ Laughing ] That's the best.

00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:39.926
I'm going to do a one-word
Michael Keaton impression.

00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:42.228
-Okay, okay.
-It's Michael Keaton listening

00:07:42.228 --> 00:07:44.130
and then saying yes to
what you're asking.

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:48.467
[ Laughter ]

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:51.671
Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:54.206
-Oh, my God.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:54.206 --> 00:07:56.074
-Congrats on the show.

00:07:56.074 --> 00:07:58.044
Thank you for being here.
It's always a pleasure.

00:07:58.044 --> 00:07:59.444
-Thanks for having me.
-Peter Sarsgaard.

00:07:59.444 --> 00:08:02.014
Series finale of "Dopesick"
streaming tomorrow on Hulu.

